;; The typed abort ability: `throw` / `try` / `catch` as a TRACKED control
;; effect. This is the widening `surface-status.edn` `:explicit-errors` names
;; under `:widening-path {:mechanism :typed-abort-ability}`. The invariant that
;; entry protects is not "no errors" but "no UNTRACKED control effect", and an
;; abort that appears in the inferred effect row and lowers to a result value
;; is tracked twice over.
;;
;; Lexical and one-shot, Unison-Exception-shaped but smaller: no continuation
;; capture, no general effect handlers, no unwinding mechanism at all.
{:kotoba.lang.abort-ability/version 2
 :kotoba.lang.abort-ability/status :slice-2-implemented
 :kotoba.lang.abort-ability/as-of "2026-09-02"
 :kotoba.lang.abort-ability/authority "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
 :kotoba.lang.abort-ability/implementation "kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema kotoba.compiler.frontend (elaborate-aborts)"
 :kotoba.lang.abort-ability/decided-by "adr-2608301500-guest-syntax-restrictions-classified-by-shielding-mechanism"
 :kotoba.lang.abort-ability/owner-decision "2026-09-02: build it, fail-closed, in slices"

 :invariant
 "abort is a tracked control effect: it appears in the inferred row and lowers to [:result T E]; the ambient forms never exist post-elaboration"

 ;; The row member is the bare keyword. It does NOT encode E: the row says
 ;; that a function aborts, and the elaborated interface `[:result T E]` says
 ;; with what. A caller that needs E reads the callee's result type. Encoding
 ;; E in the row would make every row a type, and rows are compared as sets.
 :effect-row-member :abort
 :effect-row-member-carries-error-type false

 :semantics
 {:throw
  {:form "(throw e)"
   :static-type :bottom
   :note "Unifies with either branch of an `if`. The enclosing function acquires :abort in its inferred effect row. Its error type E is the type of `e`; every throw in one function must agree on E, refused otherwise with both types named."}
  :try
  {:form "(try body (catch binder handler))"
   :explicit-form "(try body (catch E binder handler))"
   :clauses 1
   :body-forms 1
   :handler-forms 1
   :binder-type "E: the explicit annotation, or the single error type the body can abort with -- its own throws plus the E of every aborting function it calls with no try body in between"
   :value-type "the join of the body's type and the handler's type; a branch that always aborts contributes nothing to the join"
   :effect "removes :abort from the row `body` contributed; what the handler throws leaves the try"
   :refuses ["a body that cannot abort (nothing to catch)"
             "a body that aborts with two different error types"
             "an explicit E the body does not throw"]
   :nested :admitted}
  :elaboration
  {:stage "post-desugar, after result inference, before any pass that writes HIR"
   :aborting-function "lowered to return [:result T E], where T is its source result type"
   :throw "(result-err-of [:result T E] e)"
   :plain-tail-value "(result-ok-of [:result T E] v)"
   :let-binding-that-aborts "(result-match-of [:result T' E] value name <rest> err (result-err-of [:result T E] err))"
   :try "one (result-match-of [:result T E] body ok ok binder handler) -- the handler appears once"
   :call-to-aborting-function
   {:inside-try-body "admitted; the try's E must equal the callee's E"
    :in-a-function-that-throws-the-same-E "admitted; the call's err arm re-raises"
    :anywhere-else "admitted since slice 2: the CALLER becomes aborting with the same E, its interface is lowered to [:result T E], and the call site's err arm re-raises. What is refused is disagreement about E, and the export boundary."}
   :wire-abi :unchanged
   :unwinding :none}
  :what-makes-a-function-aborting
  "Its own throws that no try body between them and the function boundary catches, AND -- since slice 2 -- the aborting functions it calls with no try body in between. E is therefore an INTERPROCEDURAL inference, computed to a fixed point alongside result inference; propagation advances one call edge per round, which terminates because the call graph is finite. Slice 1's strict reading kept E a per-function local fact and made the middle of a call chain unwritable: `parse` throws, `read` calls it, and `read` had to write a `throw` it did not mean in order to be allowed to call."

  :one-error-type-per-function
  {:rule "everything that can abort one function must agree on one E"
   :refusals {:own-throws-disagree "function f throws two different error types: X and Y"
              :own-throw-vs-callee "function f throws X but calls aborting function `g`, which aborts with Y"
              :two-callees "function f calls aborting functions with two different error types: X (`g`) and Y (`h`)"}
   :measured "The second was found by the fixed point rather than by design. A function that throws gets its E in ROUND ONE, when nothing is known about its callees, so a disagreement with a callee is only visible later; the pass therefore does not short-circuit on a function it has already given an E to. With the short-circuit in place (measured 2026-09-02) that program was admitted at inference and refused much later by elaboration, naming a scope rather than the two functions."}

  :a-normalization
  {:admits "an aborting form in an operand or a test position: (+ 1 (f x)), (if (f x) a b), (g (f x) y)"
   :how "the aborting sub-expression is hoisted into a fresh `let` binding, which is the position slice 1's elaboration already lowered -- one result-match-of whose err arm re-raises. No new lowering was written."
   :evaluation-order "(op a b) evaluates a then b, so hoisting b alone would evaluate it FIRST. Once any argument is hoisted, every argument to its LEFT that is not trivially pure is hoisted too, in order; arguments to its right stay where they are."
   :triviality "containment, not shape: a vector literal CAN hold a call ((vector-at [(g 1) 2] 0) is admitted, measured 2026-09-02) while a type, [:result :i64 :string], cannot -- which is what keeps a type out of a let binding, where it would not be a value"
   :dead-throw "a `throw` reached in an operand position makes the enclosing expression dead: (+ (h 1) (throw \"s\")) becomes (let [t (h 1)] (throw \"s\")) -- (h 1) still runs, the + does not, because it never would have"
   :runs-twice "own throws are syntax and must move BEFORE inference (require-expression-type! refuses a bottom-typed operand before the fixed point could report anything); which functions abort is only known after that fixed point, so aborting calls move after it. The two runs use disjoint synthesized-name prefixes."
   :not-reached "a map or set literal holding an aborting call: the normalizer does not descend into one. Two refusals in the elaboration are the backstop, and they say that rather than naming a rule this slice lifted. NEITHER HAS A CONFORMANCE FIXTURE -- no admitted position for such a literal was found (2026-09-02), so they are unexercised guards, not measured ones."}}

 :admitted-positions
 {:tail-position true
  :let-binding-value true
  :operand-or-test true
  :loop-init false
  :note "Slice 2 A-normalizes an operand or a test into a let binding (see :a-normalization). A loop init is not admitted, and neither is anything inside a loop body, a lazy thunk or a fn literal -- that is the :checked-lexical-facet-unwind precondition, not a position question."}

 :export-boundary
 {:aborting-function-exported :refused
  :message "unhandled abort at export boundary; catch it with try: <name>"
  :why "The interface is [:result T E]; the wire ABI does not carry that across an export yet. Note: when nothing narrows the export list (no (:export ...) and no defn-), every function is exported."}

 :fail-closed-guards
 {:precondition :checked-lexical-facet-unwind
  :throw-refused-inside ["a loop/doseq/dotimes body" "a lazy thunk" "a fn literal"]
  :aborting-call-refused-inside-the-same-three
  {:since :slice-2
   :message-shape "call to aborting function `f` inside <context> is not admitted in abort slice 1: precondition :checked-lexical-facet-unwind is not met"
   :why "A throw there is refused while it is still syntax. An aborting CALL is not visible then -- which functions abort is not known until the fixed point -- so it is refused when that is known. Without this second half, slice 2's propagation would have admitted exactly what slice 1's guard was written to stop: the loop body becomes an aborting helper and the caller's try catches it, which is the silent widening measured under :measured below."
   :how "a SYNTHESIZED function (no :source-name) that would acquire E from a call rather than from its own throw; the context is read from :loop-helper? and :lazy-thunk?"
   :slice-1-wording-is-deliberate "the message still says \"abort slice 1\". It names the slice that RECORDED the precondition, and the precondition has not moved."}
  :refused-in-any-function-whose-row-has "a :dataspace/* capability (facet-enter!/facet-leave!), for throw AND for try; the row is interprocedural, so a facet operation in a callee counts"
  :message-shape "... is not admitted in abort slice 1: precondition :checked-lexical-facet-unwind is not met"
  :measured "With the loop guard removed, kotoba-sema admitted a throw inside a loop body -- the loop helper became an aborting function and the caller's try caught it. That is the silent widening the precondition exists to stop; the guard is landed and its removal turns the suite red."
  :throw-outside-any-function :refused-by-the-top-level-form-rule}

 :profiles
 {:pure-product :refused
  :why "the sugar is :backends #{:compiler}; the pure-product profile requires the wasm32-kotoba-v1 backend as well"}

 :error-codes
 #{:kotoba.error/abort-shape
   :kotoba.error/abort-precondition
   :kotoba.error/abort-error-type
   :kotoba.error/abort-value-type
   :kotoba.error/abort-nothing-to-catch
   :kotoba.error/abort-position
   :kotoba.error/abort-unhandled-call
   :kotoba.error/abort-unhandled-export
   :kotoba.error/abort-result}

 :conformance
 {:manifest "lang/conformance/manifest.edn"
  :positive [:abort-slice-1-throw-and-try-in-one-function
             :abort-slice-1-abort-through-a-callee
             :abort-slice-1-explicit-catch-type
             :abort-slice-2-propagate-through-caller
             :abort-slice-2-operand-position
             :abort-slice-2-ex-info-round-trip]
  :negative [:abort-slice-1-negative-two-error-types
             :abort-slice-2-negative-propagated-abort-at-export
             :abort-slice-1-negative-unhandled-at-export
             :abort-slice-1-negative-throw-inside-loop
             :abort-slice-1-negative-throw-in-facet-scope
             :abort-slice-1-negative-nothing-to-catch
             :abort-slice-2-negative-propagated-error-type-mismatch
             :abort-slice-2-negative-two-callees-disagree
             :abort-slice-2-negative-aborting-call-inside-loop
             :abort-slice-2-negative-throw-in-fn-literal-operand]
  :retired {:abort-slice-1-negative-call-outside-try
            "the program is the same and it is still refused, but at the EXPORT boundary now, because the call it used to be refused at is admitted. Replaced by :abort-slice-2-negative-propagated-abort-at-export, which pins the new message on the same source."}
  :pinned-by :exact-rejection-message
  :executed-by "test/kotoba/lang/abort_conformance_test.clj (KIR), kotoba-sema test/kotoba/compiler/abort_ability_test.clj"
  :not-here
  {"ex-info with non-document data"
   "refused -- \"expression type mismatch: expected document, got string\" -- but by the type checker, with code :kotoba.error/subset-reject. Every case in this set carries a :kotoba.error/abort-* code and abort_conformance_test asserts that, so admitting this one would have meant weakening the assertion that makes the set mean something. It is asserted in test/kotoba/lang/clojure_core_compat_test.clj instead, where it belongs: it is a fact about the shim's signature, not about the ability."}}

 :backends
 {:compiler :slice-2-implemented
  :kotoba-kir :executes-the-lowered-result-values
  :wasm32 :not-qualified
  :native :not-qualified
  :note "No backend needs a new construct: the lowering is result-ok-of / result-err-of / result-match-of, which every backend that admits [:result T E] already carries. Qualification is measuring that, not building it."}

 :error-type
 {:any-value-type true
  :measured "E is whatever the throw's operand types as; nothing restricts it to a string or an i64. A `:document` was measured end to end 2026-09-02 against kotoba-sema f7c56ab3: (throw (ex-info \"empty input\" (document-map ...))) gives the throwing function [:result :i64 :document] and the row #{:abort}, a bare `catch` binds the document, an explicit (catch :document e ...) pins it, and the document survives slice-2 propagation through a caller that writes no throw. NO frontend work was needed."
  :shim "lang/compat/clojure/core.kotoba provides ex-info / ex-message / ex-data on that E. It returns a DOCUMENT, not a Throwable; lang/compat.edn states the divergences (:equivalence :document-value-not-a-throwable) and the test asserts them against clojure.core rather than describing them."}

 :remaining
 [{:item "typed abort across an export boundary as a [:result T E] interface"
   :status :not-done
   :note "the ABI must carry the result shape for the export; until then the abort is refused at the boundary. Slice 2 makes this the FIRST refusal most programs meet, because propagation now carries an uncaught abort all the way out."}
  {:item "throw inside loop/doseq/dotimes bodies, lazy thunks, fn literals, and facet scopes"
   :status :not-done
   :blocked-on :checked-lexical-facet-unwind
   :note "slice 2 extended the guard to aborting CALLS in the same three contexts; the contexts themselves are still closed"}
  {:item "aborting forms in loop-init position, and inside a map or set literal"
   :status :not-done
   :note "operand and test position are done (:a-normalization). A loop init reaches the loop-helper call's argument list, which is an operand position of a synthesized call -- untested. A map or set literal is what the normalizer does not descend into; no admitted position for one holding an aborting call was found, so the elaboration's two backstop refusals are unexercised."}
  {:item "wasm32 / native qualification of the lowered programs"
   :status :not-measured
   :note "unchanged by slice 2: the lowering is still result-ok-of / result-err-of / result-match-of. A :document E adds the document value type to what a backend must carry, which wasm32 already does."}
  {:item "the elaboration's two backstop refusals have never fired for the reason they name"
   :status :unexercised
   :note "abort-position and abort-unhandled-call in elaborate-aborts. Both were reachable in slice 1 and are not now; they are kept fail-closed rather than removed, and this entry is here so the set is not read as measured."}]}
